    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0237",
      "sequence": 249,
      "meaning": "Praising Sītā’s charm in their hearts, the brothers returned to their guru. Rāma told Kauśika everything, for his nature was simple and untouched by concealment. Receiving the flowers, the sage performed worship and then blessed both brothers: ‘May your desires bear fruit.’ Rāma and Lakṣmaṇa were glad to hear it. After eating, the discerning sage began telling an old story. When day had passed, the brothers received the guru’s permission and went to perform the evening rite. The beautiful moon rose in the east; seeing it resemble Sītā’s face gave Rāma joy, but he reconsidered inwardly: the moon was not equal to her countenance. Born from the ocean, kin to poison, pale by day and blemished, how could the poor, destitute moon equal Sītā’s face?",
