    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0238",
      "sequence": 250,
      "meaning": "‘It wanes and waxes, brings pain to separated lovers, and Rāhu devours it when he finds the conjunction. It pains the cakravāka birds and is hostile to lotuses—the moon, you have many faults.’ To compare it with Vaidehī’s face would create a grave fault and be most improper. Having praised Sītā’s beauty through the pretext of the moon, Rāma returned to the guru when he realized the night was advanced. The brothers bowed to the sage’s lotus feet, received leave and rested. When night passed, Raghunātha awoke, looked at his brother and said, ‘Dear one, behold the red dawn, giver of joy to lotuses, cakravākas and the world.’ Joining both hands, Lakṣmaṇa replied in gentle words that intimated the Lord’s power: ‘At sunrise the water-lilies close and the stars’ light grows dim, just as kings have grown powerless on hearing of your arrival.’",
