    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0239",
      "sequence": 251,
      "meaning": "‘All those kings, like stars, make a little light but cannot remove the deep darkness of the bow. Lotuses, cakravākas, bees and birds of many kinds all rejoice when night ends; in the same way, Lord, all your devotees will rejoice when the bow is broken. The sun rose and effortlessly destroyed the darkness; the stars hid and the world’s radiance appeared. Through the pretext of its rising, Raghurāya, the sun showed all the kings your majesty, disclosed the glory of your arm’s strength, and revealed the way the bow would be broken.’ Hearing his brother, the Lord smiled. They bathed in natural purity, performed the daily rites, approached the guru and bowed their handsome heads at his lotus feet. Janaka then summoned Śatānanda and immediately sent him to Kauśika. Śatānanda delivered Janaka’s respectful invitation; the sage gladly called the brothers. The Lord bowed at Śatānanda’s feet and sat near the teacher. ‘Come, dear ones,’ the sage then said, ‘Janaka has sent for us.’",
